<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 2.1: Traversing Circles</title>

  <assemblage>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><term>Midline:</term> the <em>line</em> of form <q><m>y=m</m></q> that cuts the circle in half vertically</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Amplitude:</term> the distance from the midline to the highest point</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Periodic:</term> a function that repeats itself exactly forever, (<m>f(x+k) = f(x)</m> for some <m>k</m>)</p></li>
        <li><p><term>Period:</term> the smallest number <m>p</m> such that the function repeats itself exactly (<m>f(x+p)=f(x)</m>)</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.1.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>A weight is placed on a frictionless table next to a wall and attached to a spring that is fixed to the wall. From its natural position of rest, the weight is imparted an initial velocity that sets it in motion. The weight then oscillates back and forth, and we can measure its distance, <m>h=f(t)</m>
 (in inches) from the wall at any given time, <m>t</m>
 (in seconds). A graph of <m>f(t)</m>
 and a table of select values are given below.</p>
      <sidebyside widths="25% 25% 35%">
        <tabular bottom="minor">
          <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>8.000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.25</cell>
            <cell>6.087</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.5</cell>
            <cell>4.464</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.75</cell>
            <cell>3.381</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>3.000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1.25</cell>
            <cell>3.381</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1.5</cell>
            <cell>4.464</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1.75</cell>
            <cell>6.087</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>8.000</cell>
          </row>

        </tabular>
        <tabular bottom="minor">
          <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
          </row>
   
          <row>
            <cell>2.25</cell>
            <cell>9.913</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>2.5</cell>
            <cell>11.536</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>2.75</cell>
            <cell>12.619</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>13.000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3.25</cell>
            <cell>12.619</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3.5</cell>
            <cell>11.536</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3.75</cell>
            <cell>9.913</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>8.000</cell>
          </row>

        </tabular>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>A periodic function f(t) representing the weight attached to a spring fixed to the wall.</shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-2-1-3">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="15">
                          <definition> f(x) = -5*sin((2*pi)/4*x)+8 </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-1,-1,14,14]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="t" ylabel="h=f(t)" decorations="yes"
                                spacings="((0,1,14),(0,1,14))"/>
                                <graph function="f" />


                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Determine the period <m>p</m>,
 midline <m>y=m</m>,
 and amplitude <m>a</m>
 of the function <m>f(t)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          Period <m>p=4</m> since that's how long the behavior takes to 
          repeat exactly. The midline <m>y=8</m>. The amplitude is <m>5</m>. (All of these 
          are by definition.)
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the greatest distance the weight is displaced from the wall? What is the least distance the weight is displaced from the wall? What is the range of <m>f</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          The greatest distance is 13 inches from the wall, the smallest 
          distance is 3 inches from the wall.  Thus the range is <m>[3,13]</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Determine the average rate of change of <m>f</m>
 on the intervals <m>[4,4.25]</m>
 and <m>[4.75,5]</m>.
 Write one careful sentence to explain the meaning of each (including units). In addition, write a sentence to compare the two different values you find and what they together say about the motion of the weight.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          First interval:  (4 seconds has the same output as 0 seconds, and 
          <m>t=4.25</m> has the same output as <m>t=0.25</m> seconds, and 
          <m>4.75</m> as <m>0.75</m>, ...
          because the period is 4.  So a shift of 4 seconds repeats exactly what 
          happened 4 seconds ago.)
          <md>
            AV_{[4, 4.25]} = \frac{f(4.25)-f(4)}{4.25-4}=\frac{6.087-8}{0.25}=-7.652\frac{in}{s}.
          </md>
          Second interval:
          <md>
            AV_{[4.75,5]} = \frac{f(5)-f(4.75)}{5-4.75}=\frac{3-3.381}{0.25}=-1.524\frac{in}{s}.
          </md>
          Both are change in positions, or speeds.  However the speed near the midline
          <m>y=4</m> is much faster than the speed near the bottom of the motion. We can
          also see this in the slope of the graph near the midline and near the lowest points.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Based on the periodicity of the function, what is the value of <m>f(6.75)</m>?
 of <m>f(11.25)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          Period 4, so <m>f(6.74) = f(6.75 - 4) = f(2.75) = 12.619</m>. And 
          <m>{f(11.25) = f(11.25-4*2) = f(3.25) = 12.619}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.1.4</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the same setting as Activity 2.1.3: a weight oscillates back and forth on a frictionless table with distance from the wall given by, <m>h=f(t)</m>
 (in inches) at any given time, <m>t</m>
 (in seconds). The same graph of <m>f(t)</m>
 and a table of select values are given below.</p>
            <sidebyside widths="25% 25% 35%">
        <tabular bottom="minor">
          <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>8.000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.25</cell>
            <cell>6.087</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.5</cell>
            <cell>4.464</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>0.75</cell>
            <cell>3.381</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>3.000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1.25</cell>
            <cell>3.381</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1.5</cell>
            <cell>4.464</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>1.75</cell>
            <cell>6.087</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>8.000</cell>
          </row>

        </tabular>
        <tabular bottom="minor">
          <row bottom="major">
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
          </row>
   
          <row>
            <cell>2.25</cell>
            <cell>9.913</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>2.5</cell>
            <cell>11.536</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>2.75</cell>
            <cell>12.619</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>13.000</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3.25</cell>
            <cell>12.619</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3.5</cell>
            <cell>11.536</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>3.75</cell>
            <cell>9.913</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>8.000</cell>
          </row>

        </tabular>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>A periodic function f(t) representing the weight attached to a spring fixed to the wall.</shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-2-1-4">
                        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="15">
                          <definition> f(x) = -5*sin((2*pi)/4*x)+8 </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-1,-1,14,14]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="t" ylabel="h=f(t)" decorations="yes"
                                spacings="((0,1,14),(0,1,14))"/>
                                <graph function="f" />


                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of an interval of length <m>0.25</m>
 units on which <m>f</m>
 has its most negative average rate of change. Justify your choice.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          Know from previous activity that the weight is fastest
          near the midline, and if it is also decreasing there i.e. going 
          down instead of up then
          it is the most negative.  These are around intervals <m>{[0,0.25]}</m>, 
          or <m>{[4,4.25]}</m>, or <m>{[8,8.25]}</m>, or ....
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the longest interval you can find on which <m>f</m>
 is decreasing.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          If <m>f</m> is decreasing it is going down, or getting smaller.  If we start 
          at the highest value and continue until the lowest value, it is decreasing that entire
          time.  So the intervals (on <m>t</m>) are:
          <m>{(3,5)}</m>, or <m>{(7,9)}</m>, or <m>{(11,13)}</m>, or ....
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of an interval on which <m>f</m>
 is curving up (or speeding up).</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          It's getting faster, or curving up (looks like a smiley face) when 
          it's below the midline!  So intervals 
          <m>{(0,2)}</m>, or <m>{(4,6)}</m>, or <m>{(8,10)}</m>, or ....
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>On an interval where <m>f</m>
 is both decreasing and curving down (slowing down), what does this tell us about how the weight is moving on that interval? For instance, is the weight moving toward or away from the wall? is it speeding up or slowing down?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          Curving down (or a frowny face) happens above the midline.  So 
          the intervals that are both above the midline and also going down (decreasing) are:
          <m>{(3,4)}</m> or <m>{(7,8)}</m> or <m>{(11,12)}</m> or .... The weight is moving 
          towards the wall but getting faster (negative, but still faster).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What general conclusions can you make about the average rate of change of a circular function on intervals near its highest or lowest points? about its average rate of change on intervals near the function’s midline?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          Near the midline it has the greatest magnitude (fastest speed), and 
          near the top and bottom points it has the slowest rate of change.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
